{"type":"message","id":"02fe8bce","parentId":"d3c4f53e","timestamp":"2026-06-05T01:20:01.756Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Wait, the old orders are back! The manual_trade cancel command only cancelled using `cancel_all_orders` which does `DELETE /fapi/v1/algoOpenOrders {\"symbol\": BNB}` and `{\"symbol\": DOT}`. But apparently the orders still exist. Let me look at what happened...\n\nActually wait - look at the old DOT orders: \"TAKE_PROFIT_MARKET SELL @ 1.110 qty=187.1\" - this is still there with status NEW. The cancel apparently didn't work for this one. \n\nAnd the DOT qty=0 orders are also still there: \"STOP_MARKET BUY @ 1.040 qty=0\" and \"TAKE_PROFIT_MARKET BUY @ 0.983 qty=0\".\n\nBut now we also have the new correct ones: \"STOP_MARKET BUY @ 1.040 qty=195.3\" and \"TAKE_PROFIT_MARKET BUY @ 0.983 qty=195.3\".\n\nWait, but the BNB orders are also back? Didn't we cancel all BNB orders? Something is very wrong.\n\nOh wait, I bet the cancel only deleted the regular open orders (limit orders), not the algo orders! Let me check what `cancel_all_orders` actually does.\n\nLooking at the code:\n```python\ndef cancel_all_orders(symbol):\n    return request(\"DELETE\", \"/fapi/v1/algoOpenOrders\", {\"symbol\": symbol})\n```\n\nHmm, this sends to `/fapi/v1/algoOpenOrders` which IS the algo orders cancel endpoint. But the Binance API endpoint for canceling all algo orders is probably different from what we're using.\n\nActually, looking at the Binance API docs, the correct endpoint to cancel all algo orders for a symbol is:\n`DELETE /fapi/v1/allOpenAlgoOrders` or `DELETE /fapi/v1/algoOpenOrders`\n\nLet me check... Actually I think the issue is that `DELETE /fapi/v1/algoOpenOrders` might require a specific header or the symbol parameter format might be different. OR the function did successfully delete them, BUT then the monitor/main loop re-created them!\n\nWait, that can't be right for BNB - BNB has no position, so the monitor shouldn't be re-creating BNB orders.\n\nUnless... the monitor is currently running and the cancellation triggered something. Or the cancellation never actually happened because maybe the function name is wrong.\n\nLet me check: `cancel_all_orders` calls `request(\"DELETE\", \"/fapi/v1/algoOpenOrders\", {\"symbol\": symbol})`.\n\nActually, let me try to cancel individual BNB orders using the algoId approach.\n\nWait, actually let me re-read the manual_trade.py cancel command:\n\nLooking at the code I read earlier:\n```\ndef cmd_cancel():\n    import binance_auto_trade as bat\n    sym = sys.argv[1]\n    bat.cancel_all_orders(sym)\n```\n\nThis calls `cancel_all_orders` which does `request(\"DELETE\", \"/fapi/v1/algoOpenOrders\", {\"symbol\": symbol})`.\n\nBut wait, looking at binance_auto_trade.py more carefully:\n```python\ndef cancel_algo_orders(symbol: str):\n    \"\"\"取消某币种全部 Algo 条件单（TP/SL）\"\"\"\n    return request(\"DELETE\", \"/fapi/v1/algoOpenOrders\", {\"symbol\": symbol})\n\ndef cancel_all_orders(symbol):\n    return request(\"DELETE\", \"/fapi/v1/algoOpenOrders\", {\"symbol\": symbol})\n```\n\nBoth functions are the same! So they call the same endpoint.\n\nBut the orders are still there. This means either:\n1. The DELETE endpoint didn't actually delete them (maybe the Binance API changed?)\n2. The monitor re-created them immediately after deletion\n3. The response indicated success but the orders weren't actually deleted\n\nThe first time I ran `manual_trade.py cancel BNB`, it said \"✅ BNB 所有挂单已撤销\". But clearly the orders are still there. This is weird.\n\nWait, let me re-check the manual_trade.py cancel command. Maybe it checks a different endpoint than what I think.\n\nLooking again at manual_trade.py, I only saw the beginning. Let me read the cancel command more carefully.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"奇怪，旧单还在。说明 cancel 命令没删到 algo 单。让我用 algoId 精确删掉。"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_00_iRugRcs6i05IM4ndGwVH2493","name":